"""
Critical angle explorer for a step-index optical fiber (core / cladding / air),
built around EJ-160 (Eljen Technology scintillating fiber), read into a
cylindrical PMMA disk (puck).

ONE set of sliders (n1_ref, n2_ref, n3_ref = core/cladding/disk index at the
589.3nm sodium D-line, plus air_gap_mm) drives everything below.

REAL geometry (confirmed): the disk is a puck with the fiber running through
its central axis. Only the LAST 18mm of the fiber is actually embedded
inside the puck - the cladding's outer surface is in direct contact with
disk (PMMA) material there, not air. Fiber and disk end together at the
same flat far face; beyond that face is open air to the camera/eye.

This makes core and clad light behave very differently:
  - CORE light never touches the cladding's outer surface, so it's
    completely unaffected - it goes straight from core to air in one step
    at the tip (no intermediate disk medium for core light at all).
  - CLAD light bounces via TIR at the cladding wall every ~1-3mm along the
    fiber - so over the 18mm embedded stretch it hits that wall many times
    before reaching the tip. Since n3~=n2 (near-matched materials), that
    contact barely reflects at all, so clad light escapes SIDEWAYS into the
    disk bulk at roughly wherever its bounce pattern first enters the
    embedded region (modeled as uniformly random along that length, since
    tracking the exact bounce phase would require simulating the full ~1m
    of upstream fiber) - then travels straight through the remaining disk
    bulk to the far face, then refracts into air like normal.

Physics recap - four interfaces, two different Snell's law forms:
  - core -> cladding (cylindrical, cosine relation):  cos(theta_c) = n2/n1
  - clad -> disk (cylindrical, cosine relation):       cos(theta_c) = n3/n2
  - clad/disk -> air (flat, sine relation):            sin(theta_c) = 1/n
  These are all the same underlying n1*sin(theta1)=n2*sin(theta2) or
  n1*cos(theta1)=n2*cos(theta2) Snell's law, evaluated at their own
  boundary case.

n1_ref/n2_ref/n3_ref represent "what if EJ-160's real indices at 589.3nm
were these, instead of Eljen's published 1.59/1.49 (and disk matched to
cladding)" - moving them rescales a literature polystyrene/PMMA dispersion
SHAPE (Sultanova et al.) to match, since Eljen only publishes single index
numbers, not a full dispersion curve. This is an assumption worth
remembering: only the 589.3nm point is anchored to real data, everything
else follows from the borrowed dispersion shape.

Spectrum vs fiber length: the wavelengths of the simulated rays are drawn from
MEASURED EJ-160 spectra - the 19-position "EJ-160 With Carrier - Light
Attenuation by Position" data (0.124m to 2.799m, digitized from the plot into
EJ160_CARRIER_INTENSITY), linearly interpolated in position between the
nearest two measured curves (lengths outside that range hold the nearest
measured curve). The fiber_length_m slider therefore reshapes the spectrum
exactly as measured. Only each curve's SHAPE is used: overall intensity loss
comes from the attenuation sliders (core A, clad A), and no attenuation
lengths from any other source are built in. The external LED (~427nm) sits in
the WLS dye's absorption band and is re-emitted at the dye's own spectrum
almost immediately (mm-scale absorption length), so the measured spectrum is
what propagates; the LED data further down is kept for reference only.

On top of that, the radius-dependent core/clad effect comes purely from the
ray-trace geometry (wavelength-dependent critical angles and escape paths) -
core and clad rays share the same source spectrum at a given length, and
differ through the geometry, not through a separate clad spectrum.

SIDE ILLUMINATION (default): the LED shines across the core at the chosen length;
the dye absorbs it over "LED absorb. len (mm)" and re-emits along the beam path,
so emitters sit on the LED side of the core. Untick "side illumination" to get
the concentric-annulus emission below. The measured spectra were taken this way,
so the wavelength input is unchanged.

FRONT ILLUMINATION (tick "front illum."): LED shining down the core, so the light
never crosses the cladding/core boundary from the side. The second ~525 nm hump
is then absent (seen experimentally): the spectrum is the 0.124 m shape with the
second hump removed (single hump at ~500 nm, independent of length), and emitters
follow the annulus sliders (uniform by default).

Concentric emission + skew rays: the "emit inner/outer r/R" sliders place
the emitters in an annulus (0..1 = uniform over the core, e.g. 0.8..1.0 = outer
ring only). With "include skew rays" on, trapping depends on start radius
(edge emitters couple ~2x more light than centre ones); off = old
meridional-only behaviour. Plot 9 shows trapping vs emission radius.
Plot 10 is the near-field end-face image (light per pixel / centre) plus the
edge-ring/centre ratio vs LED distance, like the skew-ray deck's slide 24.
Plot 11 isolates the wavelength spectrum of skew-only light (core-trapped only
thanks to skew) vs meridional core light vs cladding light.
Plot 12 = attenuation length vs wavelength fitted from your carrier data. The
"clad light unshifted" checkbox draws cladding-ray wavelengths from the 0.124 m
(unshifted) spectrum, on the idea that the second ~525 nm hump comes from
cladding light being re-emitted by the core dye on its way into the core.

Run this whole cell in Google Colab. ipywidgets ships natively - no install
needed. Sliders update on release (not every drag pixel) to keep the ray
trace responsive.
"""

